-1204-5999-8bf7-82a35ff7ac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{(s-5)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441271} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c1623d-014a-50f3-b424-4e1e379964fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s-5)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03995eb-f98e-5978-b4e2-7edc67535e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s+5)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441267} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef7b2b3d-623b-59d8-9ef8-e0b4f659d6e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "526e692c-07a8-5c67-8d8e-821cf2ff406c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331797" :question/problem "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s+5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054b45a4-571c-51d5-8916-deb99bc5b866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302492} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca22da3-5ee1-5636-8493-a9e95b0feb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302938} {:answer/id #uuid "99cc8503-b394-5afa-bcf3-b92224bdd7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303409} {:answer/id #uuid "d181ebc5-ac4e-53ee-a2b6-83f29a3544fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303748} {:answer/id #uuid "d55441dc-9527-50c3-8c30-0068b0b8c212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d181ebc5-ac4e-53ee-a2b6-83f29a3544fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303748} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b950977-e300-5887-b9b0-d9617038a686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a494f0da-488f-5d2c-a912-87d73ea366c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332059" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aca0833-9adf-5c73-a1ab-d07568183f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355332} {:answer/id #uuid "8290279a-5ab8-57ac-9792-73f13640f201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355812} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0198a4-4fab-5362-bb17-7ad99d588499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355926} {:answer/id #uuid "91c23727-f24d-5c74-bf75-ea326ecfa263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355953} {:answer/id #uuid "d05463d4-fa4b-567c-9712-d946fd6321f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05463d4-fa4b-567c-9712-d946fd6321f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356485} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1a231c-22ee-57a9-969d-ae5213d3dae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94d7e94a-1d17-5470-a293-1353d69fb49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89592" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+L\\{2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}]}
